WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.650
I want to talk to about
how I learned Japanese.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:09.030
This is Steve Kaufman again, if you
enjoy my videos, please subscribe,

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.980
click on the bell for notifications.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.930
So, you know, how did it come
about that I learned Japanese?

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:22.830
Japanese ended up being very
influential in my life, my social

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:24.870
life, my professional career.

00:00:25.724 --> 00:00:31.455
Uh, and yet, you know, back in 1968, I
was sent to Hong Kong by the Canadian

00:00:31.455 --> 00:00:33.525
government to learn Mandarin Chinese.

00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:39.495
So I, how did I end up with Japanese
being such a dominant language in my life?

00:00:40.004 --> 00:00:44.355
So I thought I'd go over that and go over
with you some of the sort of things that

00:00:44.355 --> 00:00:51.465
helped me learn Japanese and more than
any book or anything as I sort of reflect

00:00:51.465 --> 00:00:54.015
back on it now, it was my attitude.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.970
Uh, and of course the amount
of time that I was prepared

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.430
to spend on learning Japanese.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.970
So 68, I go to Hong Kong.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.800
I spent a year learning Chinese.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:11.760
Um, and then the idea was that I would
be working in the, uh, Canadian High

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.370
Commission in Hong Kong using my Chinese.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:19.530
Unfortunately there developed
a sort of a conflict of,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.840
personality conflict or whatever, with
my immediate boss at the High Commission.

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:31.950
And the idea was that, uh, Canada would
open up a, an embassy in Beijing and

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.660
I would be working for this person.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.180
Uh, it would be a very isolated posting.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.060
He and I didn't get along.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.180
So I announced to the Canadian
Trade Commissioner Service that I

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.330
refused to go to Beijing to work

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:52.230
under this person in an
isolated post, life would be

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:53.970
hell for two and a half years.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:58.680
I said on the other hand, since the
government had paid for me, essentially

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:05.150
to learn Chinese, which I had done in,
in, uh, you know, quick time in a year,

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:09.420
whereas normally two years was sort
of the allotted time to learn Chinese.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:14.340
I said, I will, if you assign me to
Japan, I will learn Japanese on my own.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:18.045
Won't cost you a penny and you
will have at least gotten some

00:02:18.045 --> 00:02:19.455
return on your investment.

00:02:20.645 --> 00:02:24.695
Looking back on it is, is kind of,
um, almost a foolhardy thing for me to

00:02:24.695 --> 00:02:30.395
do, but I was not to go and spend two
years in an isolated post with someone

00:02:30.395 --> 00:02:35.285
with whom I had very strong personality
conflicts and in their wisdom the

00:02:35.315 --> 00:02:37.075
Trade Commissioner Service agreed.

00:02:37.685 --> 00:02:42.425
So I then started buying books
in Hong Kong on Japanese.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:50.490
And, uh, I still remember that one of the
first things I learned was, uh, how does,

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.680
how you, how you say, "how do you do?"

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.090
When you meet someone for the first time.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:03:03.900
And the expression was ... I
learned that I sort of memorized it.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:05.940
I have never used that expression.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.220
Nobody uses, nobody uses that expression,
a completely useless expression.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.630
It's an indication that much of
what you get in beginner books

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.880
may not be all that useful.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:20.820
You have to be prepared to...

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.510
you don't necessarily eliminate
it, but you learn it and forget it.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:25.380
And you don't worry about it.

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:28.920
Anything that's thrown at me
at any stage in my learning.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:33.000
It's not really important to me,
whether I am able to hang on to it.

00:03:33.435 --> 00:03:36.405
In time, I'll figure it out,
whether I need it or whether, I

00:03:36.405 --> 00:03:42.825
don't know, in the case of ... not
something that I ever needed.

00:03:43.725 --> 00:03:48.195
So while in Hong Kong, because I had
friends amongst the various other

00:03:48.195 --> 00:03:52.745
diplomats who were studying Chinese,
I had very good friends amongst the,

00:03:53.015 --> 00:03:54.995
uh, people at the Japanese consulate,

00:03:55.355 --> 00:04:00.454
and one of those people
was very fluent in Chinese.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.410
And, uh, he wanted to learn English.

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:09.929
So we would meet for lunch
twice a week at a very famous,

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.690
uh, dim sum place in Hong Kong.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:18.599
The, uh, ... Hotel and, uh,
Tuesday we would speak English and

00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:20.789
Thursday we would speak Japanese.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.360
I don't know what I was able to
say, whatever I was able to find

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.010
in my book, I would try it out.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.960
It wasn't much of a conversation.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:32.729
But it was part of starting
to experience things Japanese.

00:04:33.359 --> 00:04:40.680
So then in, uh, I guess January of
'71, we moved to Japan and I went to

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:48.630
a school right under the Tokyo tower
called the ..., uh, ... Japanese school.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:52.770
Nitoguri Sensei was a
very charismatic man.

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:56.490
And his message was ... heart to heart.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.830
And we would sit around in this classroom,
five or six of us, and we would all

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:04.020
try to mumble something in Japanese.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.090
And I did that for a month.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.580
And then I realized that this
was largely a waste of my time.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:10.110
So I quit that.

00:05:11.385 --> 00:05:14.685
And I started doing a lot of reading.

00:05:14.955 --> 00:05:19.455
I let a, I read a lot in, in this
series by Naganuma, which I've

00:05:19.455 --> 00:05:21.345
mentioned before, which is all Hiragana.

00:05:21.885 --> 00:05:26.185
Because since I had the characters from
Chinese, I had to get used to the kanna,

00:05:26.565 --> 00:05:30.975
and even though compared to Chinese
characters, Hiragana is easy to learn,

00:05:30.975 --> 00:05:33.255
there's only whatever it is, 50 symbols.

00:05:33.915 --> 00:05:38.325
Never underestimate how difficult it
is to get used to a new writing system,

00:05:38.655 --> 00:05:42.645
even though theoretically, you know
what the symbols represent, before

00:05:42.645 --> 00:05:48.015
your brain gets used to absorbing, you
know, messages, information with that

00:05:48.015 --> 00:05:50.355
writing system takes a long, long time.

00:05:50.355 --> 00:05:57.015
So I read lots and I listened to lots
and continued to scour the bookstores

00:05:57.015 --> 00:06:02.925
for reading material, wherever possible
with cassette tapes, much more difficult

00:06:03.015 --> 00:06:06.975
at that time to find such material,
but whatever I could find I consumed.

00:06:07.605 --> 00:06:12.495
I was lucky enough that, uh, at the
Canadian Embassy where I was working, I

00:06:12.495 --> 00:06:17.265
had an assistant commercial, uh, officer,
as they were known, these sort of locally

00:06:17.265 --> 00:06:21.705
engaged staff who worked with the trade
commissioners, who had the contacts,

00:06:21.795 --> 00:06:23.475
who would accompany you to visits.

00:06:23.835 --> 00:06:30.284
And his name was Nick Yazaki and he was extremely
long-winded, and extremely patient and

00:06:30.284 --> 00:06:33.765
he would speak to me and he would say
everything four or five times, and I would

00:06:34.185 --> 00:06:38.895
listen to him and I would imitate his,
the way he droned on and repeated things.

00:06:38.895 --> 00:06:41.895
But when you're learning a language,
if you are lucky enough to have

00:06:41.895 --> 00:06:46.065
someone to talk to who is, uh,
you know, long of wind and repeats

00:06:46.065 --> 00:06:47.924
things, that's extremely helpful.

00:06:48.555 --> 00:06:52.375
And with Nick, we would go and
visit, uh, you know, potential,

00:06:52.375 --> 00:06:56.850
uh, you know, contacts, people who
wanted to buy Canadian products.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.080
Uh, first two years I was promoting
food products in the second two years

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:05.430
of the four years, I was promoting
forest products and we would visit

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:09.510
with Japanese contacts and, um, I
would kind of hang in there, but,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.750
you know, and I try to say something
and I pretend that I understood it.

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:14.670
And I think this was the
other attitude that I had.

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:17.520
It never bothered me that I,
for the longest time, didn't

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:18.570
understand a hundred percent.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:23.940
I understand 30%, 40%, 50%, but I'd
be in there nodding part of the deal

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:26.970
after the meeting, I'd asked Nick,
like, what exactly did they say?

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:29.730
I, maybe I said something
that was totally irrelevant.

00:07:29.910 --> 00:07:31.380
It never really bothered me.

00:07:31.870 --> 00:07:34.350
Uh, I was in a Japanese environment.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.470
I was being exposed to the language
I was picking up on things.

00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:40.170
And, but it does take a long time.

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:44.340
And I can remember that even after years
of living in Japan, the only television

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:48.980
programs that I could understand where
baseball and Sumo, because there's,

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:54.290
you know, the dialogue is relatively
limited, uh, you seeing what's happening.

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:57.560
And so that was kind
of what I would watch.

00:07:57.560 --> 00:08:02.700
It took me a long time before I could
understand, you know, uh, drama, you

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:06.780
know, soap operas or sword movies or
slash movies or any of that stuff.

00:08:07.289 --> 00:08:10.440
Uh, it just takes a long
time, but I was in a hurry.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:13.710
I certainly didn't want to
write any exam in Japanese.

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:17.479
I just wanted to use it to the
extent that I could use it.

00:08:18.049 --> 00:08:22.849
And, and where my Japanese really took
off was when I, uh, left the embassy

00:08:22.849 --> 00:08:24.349
and I started working on my own.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.719
So now I didn't have a Nick Yazaki.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:33.240
I had to basically, you know, handle
these relationships by myself.

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:39.375
And, uh, there were times and I had
Japanese people working with me and

00:08:39.375 --> 00:08:45.225
we'd be in the car, driving around
the countryside for hours in traffic.

00:08:45.584 --> 00:08:49.635
And just talking, talking, talking,
talking about lumber, talking about,

00:08:49.995 --> 00:08:53.204
and of course the Japanese way
of business is quite fascinating.

00:08:53.334 --> 00:08:58.064
And I had to learn about how they cut
the trees, the logs, and how they cut

00:08:58.064 --> 00:09:02.415
for different grade for different grain,
for different qualities, the different,

00:09:02.444 --> 00:09:07.275
uh, you know, idiosyncrasies of wood
that they didn't like or did like.

00:09:07.755 --> 00:09:10.410
And we could talk forever about wood.

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:15.540
Mostly we just talked about wood, but
we talked for hours and I also had

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.300
a customer, but we didn't only talk
wood, I had a customer when I was

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:21.450
working for a major, again, Canadian
forest products, export company.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.190
I had a customer in Nagoya.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:28.230
We were also selling newsprint and the
Chunichi Shimbun in Nagoya, which is the

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:30.000
major newsprint...newspaper in Nagoya,

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:35.240
and they had a purchasing manager and
I had to go and be nice to him so that

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.230
they would continue buying our paper.

00:09:38.204 --> 00:09:42.064
And I would go down there once a
month and yeah, and we would sit

00:09:42.064 --> 00:09:45.875
in a Japanese restaurant and sit on
the floor and, uh, consume a bottle

00:09:45.875 --> 00:09:48.875
of whiskey and discuss philosophy.

00:09:48.935 --> 00:09:52.175
So I had to be up on subjects
related to philosophy.

00:09:52.234 --> 00:09:54.545
We didn't talk about the price
of newpaper, we didn't talk about

00:09:54.545 --> 00:09:56.375
anything related to business.

00:09:56.375 --> 00:09:59.405
We talked about philosophy,
which was a lot of fun.

00:10:00.064 --> 00:10:02.854
And, uh, I mean, I can't remember
all the different things, but my,

00:10:02.854 --> 00:10:06.724
my basic attitude was one of just,
you know, roll with the punches.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:12.599
Um, and I would go in and find
readers with glossaries and, and for

00:10:12.599 --> 00:10:16.260
the longest time, when I saw Chinese
character, I would pronounce it

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:18.270
to myself in Chinese.

00:10:18.870 --> 00:10:22.620
And um very often not know how
that was pronounced in Japanese.

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:26.430
I, there were like, I would hear it
and I knew what it meant, but I didn't

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:30.660
necessarily relate that character
to any particular pronunciation.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:33.720
So I would read in Hiragana
and pronounce it in Chinese,

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.080
continue reading in Hiragana,

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:37.390
and acquire this vocabulary.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:42.390
Now this has all been, become
much easier today because, because

00:10:42.390 --> 00:10:46.050
we have text to speech, we can
look up words in dictionaries.

00:10:46.050 --> 00:10:49.530
We can, uh, we have, like at
LingQ we have furigana, the little

00:10:50.330 --> 00:10:51.690
guys on top of the character.

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.340
So much easier.

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:56.850
It's unbelievably much easier,
even though, you know, ta LingQ

00:10:56.850 --> 00:11:01.665
it's not perfect because the
word-splitting algorithm doesn't

00:11:01.665 --> 00:11:03.245
quite work as well as we would like.

00:11:03.245 --> 00:11:05.585
And we're looking at ways to
make it better, but it's still

00:11:05.585 --> 00:11:09.515
so much easier to learn today
than it was back in those days.

00:11:09.755 --> 00:11:14.255
So, uh, and, and I should say amongst the
various sources that I would listen to,

00:11:14.255 --> 00:11:19.385
and I got into my car and Tokyo in August
when it was like 36 degrees and the sun

00:11:19.385 --> 00:11:23.825
has been beating down on my car and I get
in there and the steering wheel is just

00:11:23.915 --> 00:11:30.675
like, scalding hot then I would put on
my ... the history of the Shōwa era, which

00:11:30.675 --> 00:11:33.285
I must have listened to 20, 30 times.

00:11:33.824 --> 00:11:40.064
So it's all I think my immersion in
Japanese was one of, I wanted to explore,

00:11:40.454 --> 00:11:42.375
I wanted to experience the language.

00:11:42.435 --> 00:11:46.395
I'd say what I thought I was able
to say, sometimes get it wrong.

00:11:46.425 --> 00:11:47.535
It didn't bother me.

00:11:47.805 --> 00:11:49.694
I listened to what
people were saying to me.

00:11:49.995 --> 00:11:54.315
And basically relied on my
brain to gradually start saying

00:11:54.315 --> 00:11:56.385
more and more things correctly.

00:11:56.655 --> 00:12:02.835
I never really focused in on the grammar,
grammar rules, rules about when to

00:12:02.835 --> 00:12:05.775
use which polite form, none of that.

00:12:05.775 --> 00:12:10.545
I just basically allowed the language to
come to me and trusted that my brain would

00:12:10.545 --> 00:12:12.525
pick up on the patterns of the language.

00:12:13.095 --> 00:12:18.015
And today my Japanese is,
as it is, it's not perfect.

00:12:18.390 --> 00:12:23.370
Uh, I don't, I haven't tried to zoom
in on any particular aspects of either

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:27.750
polite language or pitch accent or any
of these other things I communicate.

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:28.860
I understand.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:30.090
I'm happy.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:32.040
It serves my purposes.

00:12:32.370 --> 00:12:36.600
And I think people, uh, are
quite comfortable communicating

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:38.220
with me in Japanese.

00:12:38.459 --> 00:12:42.300
And you can watch two of my videos in
Japanese here, just to give you a bit

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:45.930
of the flavor of my Japanese as it is
right now, which reminds me, I just

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:50.370
wanted to say one more thing, learning
other languages and having an absence

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:52.680
from Japanese has improved my Japanese.

00:12:53.275 --> 00:12:56.115
In other words, the fact of learning
other languages and particularly the

00:12:56.125 --> 00:12:59.115
10 languages I've learned since the age
of 60, including Russian and including,

00:12:59.445 --> 00:13:03.765
you know, Korean, Ukrainian, and now
struggling with Arabic and Persian.

00:13:03.825 --> 00:13:08.115
All of that is good for the brain
and actually improves my Japanese.

00:13:08.265 --> 00:13:11.595
Although if I haven't spoken it for
awhile, I'm initially quite rusty,

00:13:11.655 --> 00:13:14.085
but then ultimately I become better.

00:13:14.325 --> 00:13:19.455
So there you have it, uh, a brief
discussion about how I learned Japanese.

00:13:19.485 --> 00:13:20.805
I hope that's helpful.

